{
  "_comment": [
    "Shared golden vectors for DayResolver. Loaded by BOTH the Android (JVM) and iOS",
    "(Swift) suites. The logical day boundary decides which day every entry belongs to,",
    "so a divergence here would silently corrupt every downstream figure.",
    "resolve: epochMillis is absolute; utcOffsetSeconds is the frame the reading was",
    "taken in, and the only frame the function has — it looks nothing up in a zone.",
    "The five instants around the daylight-saving switches appear twice, once with",
    "the offset before it and once with the offset after, because a fixed offset",
    "knows no switch. One of those pairs falls on two different logical days.",
    "firstDayOfWeekIso: languageTag is BCP-47; expected is an ISO-8601 weekday number",
    "(1 = Monday ... 7 = Sunday), the scheme Kotlin's WeekFields returns and the one",
    "Swift converts to from Calendar.firstWeekday, which counts Sunday as 1. The four",
    "regions are the ones whose week convention is settled: three that open on Monday",
    "and one that opens on Sunday, so both branches of the Swift conversion are hit.",
    "The number feeds the calendar grid's column order and the weekday histogram's bar",
    "order, so a divergence rotates one platform's week against the other's.",
    "",
    "logicalDayDiffers: the condition the entry sheet shows its note under. The",
    "reading is the composed date and time in its frame; logicalDay is the day the",
    "sheet was opened on. True means the entry counts toward another day.",
    "",
    "following calendar day (v0.86.0).",
    "The instantOnLogicalDate and calendarDate sections are gone with the functions they pinned. Both existed because the entry sheet offered hours and minutes alone: something had to place a bare time on a calendar day and then say which day that was. The sheet composes the whole reading now, so nothing in the app maps a logical day back onto an instant. The one place that still needs the inverse is the repair of pre-0.85.0 calendar entries, which keeps a private copy per platform on purpose (see LegacyDayRepair)."
  ],
